# machine-local registry — per-machine overrides stay out of git
*.local.toml

# Tracked: *.toml baselines, *.example templates, README.md. No negation is
# needed to keep them: `*.local.toml` matches a name ENDING in `.local.toml`,
# which no `*.toml.example` ever does. The `!*.toml.example` line that used to
# sit here un-ignored nothing, and cost three files their place in the OSS
# publish -- the percolate commit stages in-process rather than via `git add`
# and skipped every untracked path under this negation, so registry.toml.example,
# registry.local.toml.example and unreal.toml.example landed on disk in the
# mirror and were never committed. hardware.toml.example survived only because
# it was already tracked. Do not reintroduce it.

# --- Resolved values, root pointers, and snapshots ---
# This directory is reachable from a synced ~/.claude meta-repo through a
# symlink, so anything tracked here publishes to every peer machine. The
# discrimination that matters: the concern BASELINES (registry/hardware/
# unreal.toml) STAY TRACKED — their bodies are empty key declarations and their
# headers declare them shared. It is the resolved-value and snapshot files
# beside them that are one machine's state.
#
# Addon-registered concern files hold resolved absolute `location` values and
# are rewritten by registration on each machine, so they regenerate rather than
# needing to travel. Add one line per addon concern you register:
# <concern>.toml
#
# Root pointers — one machine's repo path. Siblings of the .doe-root the
# meta-repo's own .gitignore already anchors, and the same class. Matched by
# shape, not by name: a pointer named after a repo this operator happens to
# have registered must never need a new line here to stay out of git, because
# a pointer that travels arrives as a path from the wrong OS — a Windows drive
# letter landing on a Mac — where it resolves to nothing and the failure
# surfaces far from here.
.*-root
# The resolved interpreter NAME for this box, which is not the name that
# resolves on the next one.
.python-interpreter
# Boot/install sentinels carry a PID and a wall-clock stamp for one machine.
sentinels/
# Pre-surgery snapshots of any of the above, plus captured environment dumps
# (a saved %PATH% is this machine's, and nothing else's).
*.bak*
*.pre-*
user-path-backup-*
